.he "make (1)                     8/12/26                     make (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
make
- maintain program groups
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
make [ -f makefile ] [ -n ] [ -v ] [ target ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Make executes commands to bring a target up to date with the files it
depends on.  A target is remade if any file it depends on has been
modified more recently than the target, or if the target does not
exist at all.
.sp
With no target named on the command line, make brings the first target
in the makefile up to date.
.sp
A makefile is a sequence of entries.  The first line of an entry names
one or more targets, a colon, and the files those targets depend on.
The lines after it that begin with a tab are the commands that bring
the target up to date.  A name may appear to the left of a colon on
more than one line, and its dependencies accumulate, but only one set
of commands may be given for it.
.sp
A line whose first character is a sharp is a comment.  A line ending
in a backslash is continued on the next.  A command whose first
character is an at sign is executed without being echoed.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
MACROS
.in +5
.br
A line of the form
.sp
.nf
     name = text
.fi
.sp
defines a macro.  Afterwards $(name) is replaced by text.  A macro
whose name is one character may be written $x without the parentheses.
Macros may be defined in terms of other macros; expansion repeats
until nothing is left to expand.
.sp
Four macros are set by make itself as each command is prepared:
.sp
.nf
     $@   the full name of the target being made
     $*   the target name up to its first period
     $?   the dependencies newer than the target
     $$   a dollar sign
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.nf
     -f   read the named file instead of the default
     -n   print the commands but do not execute them
     -v   report what was read and what was decided
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
A program built from two objects, each compiled from a source file and
a common header:
.sp
.nf
     pgm : a.o b.o
          ccc a.o b.o -o pgm
     a.o : incl a.c
          ccc -c a.c
     b.o : incl b.c
          ccc -c b.c
.fi
.sp
The same thing written with a macro, and with the two objects sharing
one entry.  $* is the target name without its suffix, so the one
command line serves both:
.sp
.nf
     OBJECTS = a.o b.o
     pgm : $(OBJECTS)
          ccc $(OBJECTS) -o pgm
     $(OBJECTS) : incl
          ccc -c $*.c
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
.nf
     makefile     read first
     Makefile     read if there is no makefile
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), sh (1)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This make descends from the CP/M make written by Morris Code Works
around 1985, and replaces the make micronix shipped with, which was
more limited still.  The order in which the two default makefiles are
tried is the reverse of the old one: makefile is read first here.
.sp
There are no built-in rules and no suffix rules.  Every target that is
to be made must be named, and the command that makes it must be given.
.sp
There is no filename expansion anywhere.  A star or a question mark in
a dependency list is an ordinary character, and a list of source files
has to be written out in full - which is what the OBJECTS macro in the
example above is for.
